Now write src/main.rs to use the Windows API. Let me use GetSystemInfo and print the processor architecture. I'll use `windows_sys::Win32::System::SystemInformation::{GetSystemInfo, SYSTEM_INFO, PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE_AMD64}`. Let me check names: In windows-sys, `PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE_AMD64` is a constant in Win32_System_SystemInformation. `SYSTEM_INFO` struct. `GetSystemInfo` unsafe.

Let me write with a cfg guard so it also compiles on non-Windows? The project is configured to always build for the target via [build] target, so it only builds for Windows. But `cargo check` without target would fail? With `[build] target` set, `cargo build` defaults to windows-gnu. Good. But to be nice, guard with cfg(windows). Actually if we use cfg guards, building for host works too. But the task is cross-compile. Let me make it clean and cross-only but with a fallback for non-windows so `cargo check` on host doesn't break. I'll use `#[cfg(windows)]`.

Let me write main.rs:

```rust
#[cfg(windows)]
fn main() {
    use windows_sys::Win32::System::SystemInformation::{GetSystemInfo, SYSTEM_INFO};

    let mut info = unsafe { std::mem::zeroed::<SYSTEM_INFO>() };
    unsafe { GetSystemInfo(&mut info) };

    println!("Windows API SYSTEM_INFO:");
    println!("  processor architecture: {}", info.wProcessorArchitecture);
    println!("  number of processors   : {}", info.dwNumberOfProcessors);
    println!("  page size              : {} bytes", info.dwPageSize);
    println!("  allocation granularity : {} bytes", info.dwAllocationGranularity);
}

#[cfg(not(windows))]
fn main() {
    println!("This binary is intended for x86_64-pc-windows-gnu");
}
```

